
NOTICE: You are advised not to use Rhythia Online for uploading SSPM files as you cannot delete maps (nor do they accept valid GDPR data removal requests to do so).

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.31 - 10/17/2026 --

- AUTO MAPPER: AUTO MAP LIKE THIS MAP (a new slider, under DRAW in PATTERNS, and in MORE OPTIONS as Similar to This
  Map): how much it maps like the notes already in the map. Every bar of the song it maps is compared, by how it
  sounds (its kicks, snares, hats, melody and bass, beat by beat, and how loud it is), with the bars you've mapped,
  and a bar that sounds like one of yours takes that bar's notes over: its rhythm and its pattern, turned or mirrored
  so it joins on smoothly (changed round from how it was, unless only that way flows), and a phrase you mapped over
  several bars comes over bar after bar the same way, flowing on as it did. At 100% it does that wherever a part
  sounds like yours (refilling 30 seconds cut out of a map: 2/3 to 9/10 of the notes come from its own parts that
  sound the same; the star rating you set still comes out exactly); lower, less often, and in the other bars it only
  prefers your pattern's shape, played at the rhythm picked for there. A quantum map's parts come over quantum.
  Needs some of the song mapped already
- AUTO MAPPER: far fewer spins round the outside of the grid. Lap after lap round the edge (made up, or copied from
  a learned map that spins a lot) was the same thing over and over: now a half lap is a flourish, more costs more the
  more the map has spun lately (mixed and stream maps at 8 and 10: notes in spins 40% down to 24%, full laps 11 a
  minute down to 6)
- AUTO MAPPER: new stream shapes made for each part, from its sounds: snakes and turns along the grid's lines, crosses
  through the middle and (in a mixed map's intense parts) star streams of knight's-move hops, turning where the music
  hits and switching spin on a strong sound. Which shape goes where comes from the song: rolling hats snake, kicks
  cut through the middle, snare rolls go into star streams, a melody walks and turns with it, a bass line spins; parts
  that sound the same get the same kind of shape. The jump slides in a mixed map's jump parts use them too (they
  always went round the edge)
- AUTO MAPPER: less copy and paste: the same 6-move shape, turned or mirrored, costs more each time it's used again,
  and bars that sound like an earlier one copy its pattern less often (shapes repeated: 68% down to 59%, different
  shapes used: 32% up to 40%)
- AUTO MAPPER: streams no longer step across a diagonal where they hop (from the middle of an edge, a hop is a
  knight's move round to the far corner), as human stream maps never do

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.30 - 10/17/2026 --

- COLLABORATE: other people's edits no longer mess up yours
  - stored BEZIER nodes stay on your notes (they were kept by place in the note list, so someone adding or deleting a
    note before them moved your nodes onto other notes); a node note someone moves takes your node with it
  - when someone changed the export offset, everyone else's notes were moved by it twice; now just once, the same
    for everyone
  - when the whole map is sent again (someone joining, or the sync putting itself right), your notes stay the same
    notes, so your undo, selection, nodes and anchors carry on working
- SAVING (desktop app): like the original editor. SAVE AS opens the usual save window: pick the folder and name, and
  the map is saved as name.txt with name.ini next to it. After that, SAVE (and autosave) writes straight to those two
  files, nothing to click. A .txt map you open from your computer is saved back to where it came from, and its .ini
  is read from beside it like the original editor does

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.29 - 10/17/2026 --

- AUTO MAPPER: the star rating you set is what you get, even when the song (or the part of it you're mapping) doesn't
  have enough sounds for it. Once its own sounds are all used, it puts a note on every beat, then every half, third,
  quarter, sixth, eighth, twelfth or sixteenth of one, as spaced-out flowing streams, until it's hard enough, then
  takes notes out to land on it exactly. 15 stars on a 30 second part of a song: 15.00 (it was 7.9 to 10.3)
- AUTO MAPPER: the search for the right setting takes bigger steps where the rating hardly moves, so it doesn't run out
  of tries short of the target (9 stars on a part of a song: 9.00, it was 8.5)
- AUTO MAPPER: with Quantum, a forced high star rating makes bigger quantum moves on quick notes instead of tiny
  slides (15 stars at 100% Quantum: 15.00, it was 10)

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.28 - 10/17/2026 --

- COLLABORATE: who placed each note is now saved with the map, with everyone's colour on it. Collab on that map again
  and everyone gets their colour back, and the notes of people who aren't there show in their colours too (your own
  notes keep your usual colours; the colours show while you're collabing)
- COLLABORATE: when someone accepts your invite, a popup tells you to choose the map: CREATE NEW MAP, LOAD MAP,
  PASTE MAP or one from the map bar
- Every button is flat now (no gradient): the menus, settings, stats, spectate, collaborate (LEAVE too), the map
  player's menus and the editor. Settings > Graphics > Show Control Gradients puts the gradient back. Buttons on all
  the menu screens have nearly square corners, and the collab bar's LEAVE and INVITE look like the other buttons
- STATS looks like the other menus: the title, an EDITOR panel and a PLAYER panel with the numbers in rows, and the
  button below
- The editor backgrounds (Grid, Vaporwave, Neon Tunnel, Space Tunnel, Deep Space, Cubic) are sharp now: rendered from
  the map player's worlds at 3840x2160 instead of small pictures stretched over the screen
- UPDATES: a new version that comes out while the app is open is offered then: the desktop app checks every 10
  minutes and asks to Update Now (once per version if you pick Later); in the browser it asks to reload into it

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.27 - 10/17/2026 --

- TIMELINE: no more jumping back or restarting by itself while the song plays, mapping alone or together
  - the song's length was the browser's first guess when the file opened, which for many mp3s is too short: the song
    could be taken as over early, the timeline jumped to the end and the next play started from the beginning. The
    length is now put right once the whole song is decoded
  - the timeline followed the audio's own clock, which only moves in steps and now and then lags a little, and snapped
    back to it: now it runs on a steady clock eased towards the audio's, so it's smooth and never goes backwards (only
    a real jump, the audio stalling, is followed straight away)
  - if the browser paused its audio, the timeline ran on without sound and then jumped back; it now waits
  - SPECTATE: the timeline of the person you're watching no longer steps back when one of their updates comes late

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.26 - 10/17/2026 --

- The buttons on the main menu and the map player's menu are flat (no gradient) with nearly square corners, like the
  original editor's menu

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.25 - 10/17/2026 --

- SPEED: lots of notes no longer lag the editor. The timeline at the top drew every note as about 14 separate shapes;
  each note's box is now one ready-made picture, the notes' position markers and lines are drawn together, and boxes
  hidden under another one aren't drawn again. A very dense part (thousands of notes on screen) went from about 50ms a
  frame to about 11ms; with a custom note mesh from about 90ms to about 11ms
- SPEED: BEZIER is no longer laggy. The bezier preview was worked out again from scratch every frame (with 200 nodes,
  100ms a frame); it's now only worked out when the nodes or settings change and is otherwise one picture (about 2ms).
  The curve maths is much faster too, so DRAW is quick even with hundreds of nodes. The curves come out exactly the same
- SPEED: notes on the grid and text everywhere are drawn from ready-made pictures instead of shape by shape and letter
  by letter. A normal frame takes about a third less time, so the editor keeps up with 144Hz and 240Hz screens

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.24 - 10/17/2026 --

- AUTO MAPPER: the Quantum checkbox is now a Quantum slider, 0-100% (on the Patterns tab and in More Options). 100%
  is a fully quantum map. Lower, only some parts of the song are quantum (the more intense ones first), fewer and
  tamer the lower you go: around 10% it's just a few quick runs bent into straight or curved lines between grid notes,
  like the Bezier and Curve Bezier tools; around 50% some parts get real quantum jumps and slides, kept close to the
  grid; from about 75% most of the song does. 0% is no quantum at all. (Quantum on before: the slider starts at 100%)
- AUTO MAPPER: quantum now learns from the quantum maps you teach it, not the Ayumi example. It measures their design
  (how much is off the grid and how far out it goes, how their jumps turn, how often they slide and how long and fast
  the slides are, how much their streams stay on the grid) and averages it over all of them, then makes its own
  patterns in that design. The Ayumi example is only used when it has learned no quantum maps
- AUTO MAPPER: much less copy and paste. From a learned map it takes at most a few notes at a time (8 on the grid, 6
  in quantum) and never just carries on with the next notes of the same map, so you get the maps' feel and not their
  patterns pasted in; and repeated bars come back less often (still some, so it stays consistent with the song)

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.23 - 10/17/2026 --

- COLLABORATE: your play cursor on the grid (or your custom cursor image) no longer jumps around when someone else
  places, moves or deletes notes near where you are. It moves when you change the time or edit something yourself

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.22 - 10/17/2026 --

- COLLABORATE: map any map together, not only a new one. When they accept, you're back on the menu: create a map,
  load one, paste one or open one from the map bar, and that's the map you all work on (its song and notes go to
  everyone). CANCEL COLLAB on the menu stops it
- COLLABORATE: everyone's notes are in their own colour while you map together, so you can see who placed what (your
  own notes keep your usual colours)
- COLLABORATE: each person keeps the same colour the whole time and on everyone's screen (it could change, to orange,
  after someone clicked away to another window); and nobody is dropped for being quiet
- COLLABORATE: someone's mouse over the grid shows over the same cell on your screen, whatever shape your windows are
- COLLABORATE: a note placed at the same moment as someone else's edit now goes out straight away (it could wait a
  few seconds), and a big map goes over in parts
- Discord rich presence shows when you're collabing, and who with (desktop app)

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.21 - 10/17/2026 --

- Auto mapper, Target Stars: the map now comes out at the star rating you set, exactly (the same formula as Rhythia
  and CALCULATE SR/RP; checked against Rhythia's own server). It makes the map a little harder than asked, then takes
  out notes until it's on the rating. 1 to 15 stars, in steps of 0.1. Past what a song normally rates, its streams are
  spaced out (two cells a note) to get there
- Auto mapper, set number of notes: always exactly that many, in every mode, even more than the song has sounds for
  (the rest are spread evenly into the longest gaps)
- Auto mapper learns again: the maps it learned now lead what it makes (before, with only a few maps learned, the
  example maps that come with the editor took over). It also learns from your own maps when you save them, only the
  notes you placed, never the ones the auto mapper placed
- COLLABORATE: up to 5 people. The host can invite more from the editor (INVITE on the bar at the bottom), and they
  join the map as it is. Everyone has their own colour. You see someone's mouse only when they're in the part of the
  song you're looking at. Invites pop up in the corner, not the middle of the screen, and "Let Others Invite Me To
  Collab" (Settings, or the account window) turns them off. What others select or move never changes your selection
- SPECTATE: less text on screen

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.20 - 10/16/2026 --

- Auto mapper, streams: full circles. A stream now goes round the outside of the grid in laps (a lap or two, as
  human stream maps spin) instead of snaking through the middle with a turn every note or two, and it changes only on
  a strong sound in the song: from the middle of an edge, through the middle to the other side and on round, usually
  the other way (a figure of eight). Streams are one plan like this rather than bits of the learned maps stitched
  together, which is what made them feel random
- Auto mapper, mixed maps with Streams / Jumps under 100%: slides in the jump parts. Where the music has a quick
  figure (three to five sounds at least twice as quick as the jumps: a fill, a roll, a quick run into a change),
  those sounds are a slide: quick notes along the edge of the grid from where the jump lands, e.g. top right down the
  right side and along the bottom. A bar and its repeats get the same slide. At 100% (a jump map) there are none

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.19 - 10/16/2026 --

- COLLABORATE (in the menu, next to SPECTATE): map together, live. It lists everyone logged in; invite someone and
  they get a pop-up to accept. Then you choose the song on the create screen, it's sent to them, and you both map
  the same map at the same time: notes added, moved and deleted, timing points, bookmarks and extra objects show up
  for the other person straight away (about a twentieth of a second), and edits at the same moment don't overwrite
  each other. In orange you see their mouse, where they are in the song (on the track and the timeline), what they
  have selected and the notes they just placed. LEAVE (bottom of the editor) ends it; the map stays with both of you

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.18 - 10/16/2026 --

- Auto mapper, jump maps (and the jump parts of mixed maps): no more one long zigzag of the biggest jumps. The
  weaker beats now and then get a short jump, a cell or across a corner, with the big jumps on the strong sounds,
  as human jump maps do; still almost all jumps, never a row of short ones that turns into a slow stream

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.17 - 10/16/2026 --

- Auto mapper: a set number of notes in a quantum map now comes out exact (it could come out a few dozen short)
- Auto mapper: no more unplayable streams above difficulty 10. Very high star targets used to unlock streams of up
  to 50 notes a second, jumping a map from about 7 stars to 12; now the top speed stays at difficulty 10's (about 42
  notes a second), and a target the song can't reach makes it as hard as it goes, which the message says

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.16 - 10/16/2026 --

- SPECTATE now shows the mapper's whole editor, exactly as they see it: their panels, timeline, grid, selection,
  zoom and settings, scrolling and playing as they do, with their mouse moving over it (and a ring when they click).
  Still 5 seconds behind so it runs smoothly. STOP WATCHING (or Escape) takes you back to the list. Their editor
  only sends while someone is watching
- Auto mapper: star targets land closer (about a quarter of a star off on average, was up to a star). A faster
  stream speed a harder difficulty unlocks now comes in a few bars at a time instead of turning every drop into a
  twice-as-fast stream at once, and the other things that change at 8, 9 and 10 change gradually in between

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.15 - 10/16/2026 --

- Accounts: LOG IN at the top of the menu. Just a username and a password, no email
- SPECTATE (in the menu): watch people map, 5 seconds behind so it plays smoothly: their grid with the notes coming
  in, a timeline, their map and where they are in the song (their audio isn't shared). Anyone logged in with a map
  open shows up; turn off "Let Others Watch Me Map" (Settings, or the account window) if you'd rather not be watched
- CREDITS moved into Settings
- CALCULATE SR/RP is back (Export panel in the editor): the star rating and ranked points from Rhythia, as in the
  original editor. When Rhythia's server can't be reached, the star rating is worked out here with the same formula
- Auto mapper: the difficulty is now a target star rating (1 to 12 stars). It makes the map, rates it with Rhythia's
  star formula and makes it again harder or easier until it lands close; the stars it reached show when it's done.
  Takes a little longer than before. Very high targets may be out of reach on calmer songs

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.14 - 10/16/2026 --

Auto mapper:
- streams flow: no more sudden flicks against the way a stream is going (a sharp turn back, or one note kinking the
  other way), and a stream spins one way for a while before it turns round. Its turns land on the song's strong
  sounds
- streams hop like prom night's: where a note is left out, one block over at about a right angle, and on the stream
  goes; never further, never back the way it came
- Stream map: only streams. Jump map: only jumps, on the song's own rhythm, filling the jump rhythm wherever the
  song is busy
- new option Streams / Jumps (for mixed maps, 50% by default): the calmer parts of the song are jumps and the
  intense parts and drops are streams, with jumps wherever the music slows down. 0% makes a stream map, 100% a
  jump map
- Quantum: slides sweep in one smooth curve (no more snaking from side to side), and quick quantum runs curve on
  and then the other way instead of kinking back

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.13 - 10/16/2026 --

Auto mapper (a fourth round of maps at 8, 9 and 10 in every mode, reviewed against the human maps):
- a song keeps one style at every difficulty: a mixed map takes its lead from the same learned map at 8, 9 and 10,
  and a jump map or a quantum map never leads a mixed one
- no more fast there-and-back moves (out and straight back at stream or jump speed), which no human map has
- jump maps take turns between jump phrases and stream phrases through their intense parts, as human jump maps do,
  instead of a minute-long wall of jumps; the jump streams at 10 only go where something sounds
- streams run on through two stream beats in a row and break before the strong sounds, instead of always seven
  notes and a jump into every beat
- a quiet lead-in before a drop and a song dying away at the end are left nearly empty, as human maps leave them
- steady half-beat notes stay exactly on the beat, so a run of jumps keeps an even speed
- a bar whose sounds only half fit takes the division of a bar with the same drums that fits clearly
- Quantum: no more stretches of notes sitting just off the grid points (they looked like the 3x3 grid shifted a
  little); quantum notes are clearly off the grid

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.12 - 10/16/2026 --

Auto mapper:
- a bar with the same drums as the bars around it is mapped as busily as they are, even when another bass note
  under it makes its drums come through a little weaker (an EDM drop no longer drops to five notes every fourth bar)
- a build-up's snare roll that has more hits than the difficulty allows is thinned evenly, every other hit counting
  back from the drop, instead of in an uneven gallop
- Quantum: quick jumps that can't curve on (near the edge of the area) turn off at an angle instead of snapping
  straight back; repeats of a bar get the same slides
- streams: a copied bar no longer joins a stream with a two-cell skip, and a stream after a jump hooks off it
  rather than heading back the way the jump came

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.11 - 10/16/2026 --

Auto mapper (a third round of maps at 8, 9 and 10 in every mode, reviewed against the human maps):
- no more dead beats: a busy part that can't take every note keeps each beat as a stream (with a jump into the
  next beat), as every other note, or, when it has to, as its main sounds at the end of the bar, instead of a lone
  two-note beat between two walls of stream. 9 and 10 are different maps again in busy songs
- slow notes are real jumps in every part but a song's quietest (intros and outros): medium parts no longer creep
  a cell at a time, and a jump the difficulty has to shrink becomes a step across a corner rather than one cell
- songs whose kicks dodge the beat (breakcore) are no longer mapped a 16th note off: the drop's first hit gets its
  note, slow notes land on the beat, and calm and loud parts differ as much as in the human map
- Quantum: the notes between slides are real jumps (in the human quantum map almost every note 60-100ms apart is a
  jump), sweeping in wide curves when they're quick instead of snapping back at stream speed; quick runs arc
  smoothly without wheeling round the edge
- Jump map: jump phrases differ from one to the next in intense parts instead of one long wall of the same jumps,
  stream phrases run to their end, and 8, 9 and 10 step up more evenly
- mixed maps no longer make chains of jumps out of 1/8 notes next to faster bursts, no jump goes straight back
  where the note before came from at a join, and a beat-long hole in fast music gets filled

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.10 - 10/16/2026 --

Auto mapper (a second round of maps at difficulty 8, 9 and 10 in every mode, each one checked note by note
against human maps of the same songs):
- streams follow the song: a stream fills in at most one step finer than the song's own sounds, and only where
  they really sit on the beat, so a drop of eighth notes no longer becomes a wall of notes two or four times as
  fast as anything in the music. Real rolls and fast arpeggios still get their streams
- jumps into the beat: outside the drops, the note just before each sound on the beat is left out, so the beat
  gets a jump into it with stream steps in between (the way human maps play a verse's groove); in drops a stream
  runs on for a bar or so before its jump
- a busy part that can't take every note is thinned by whole beats: the strongest beats are streams all the way
  through and the rest keep their main sounds, instead of streams with notes missing here and there
- timing: sounds that all come a few milliseconds early or late still count as on the beat, so a build-up's fast
  snare roll gets every note; no stray triplet notes in straight songs; a flam (a weak hit just before a strong
  one) is one hit; a slow note only moves off the beat when its sound really is off it
- jump speed follows the difficulty: no more walls of big jumps at 10, and 8 and 9 no longer get faster jumps than
  10 (a learned pattern, or a turned stretch, now stops before a jump too quick for the difficulty)
- on fast, busy songs at 9 and 10 the calmer parts stay calmer than the drops (before, every busy part came out the
  same); a near-silent break before a drop stays a break
- Jump map: two-bar jump phrases with stream phrases between them about a third of the time, and at 9 and 10 the
  intense parts' jump phrases are jump streams; jumps out of a stream turn on like a star instead of snapping back
  onto the notes just played, and made-up streams no longer turn square corners
- Quantum: like human quantum maps, the slides are the streams and the notes between them are jumps (star jumps
  that change which way they spin now and then), while really quick notes curve along in arcs instead of
  scribbling on one spot, and a circle no longer goes round lap after lap; slides hook back round off the jump into
  them, stay on the grid's edge and curve in before it instead of folding back on themselves; the parts with
  slides are chosen from the song (the same at 8, 9 and 10), with more slides the harder the map and few at the
  easy end; calm parts get smaller jumps; 8, 9 and 10 now step up evenly
- a bar copied from the one just before it is turned or mirrored, never the same bar twice in a row

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.9 - 10/16/2026 --

Auto mapper (tested on difficulty 8, 9 and 10 in every mode against human maps of the same songs, and on a
made-up song where every drum hit is known):
- time and distance: a note that comes after a longer gap than the quick notes around it is a real jump (two
  cells or more), turning on like a star from the jump before, never a right angle or a little one-cell step.
  Slow notes are jumps across the grid instead of creeping a cell at a time, and the quick notes of a stream
  stay single steps in every style
- smoother where it should be: no more notes on the same spot twice in a row, almost no lone sharp turns in a
  flowing line, no jumping straight back and forth (A to B to A), and the joins between patterns (and where a
  repeated part starts) turn like the rest of the map instead of snapping back
- the rhythm follows the song: straight songs no longer flip to triplet streams every other bar, quiet parts
  aren't filled with streams, the verse gets fewer notes than the drop, and notes sit on the song's real sounds
  far more often
- difficulty steps evenly: a faster kind of stream comes in at short bursts at one difficulty, runs longer at
  the next and freely at 10, so 8, 9 and 10 are all different (before, 9 and 10 were often almost the same)
- jumps follow the difficulty: as fast as the streams at 10, slower below
- repeated parts come back (turned or mirrored) about as often as in human maps, even when the repeat only
  sounds alike, but a loop-based song no longer gets the same bar again and again through a whole drop
- Jump map makes jumps on the song's own rhythm with fewer filler streams; Stream map keeps stepping through
  slower notes instead of turning them into jump chains
- Quantum: no more half-cell grid snakes (a tech map's grid patterns counted as quantum before); quantum jumps
  are as big as their timing allows, so quick notes become small quantum steps instead of full jumps at stream
  speed; the note after a slide is always a jump; slides come in different lengths, go more often before strong
  beats, bend back in off the edges and keep to the band just outside the grid
- bigger moves in loud parts and drops, smaller in calm parts
- Difficulty hint updated: 10 is about 10-20 notes a second, as many as the song's sounds give

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.8 - 10/16/2026 --

Auto mapper:
- maps are laid out like a plan: a part of the song that sounds the same as an earlier one (a loop coming round, a
  chorus coming back) gets exactly the same rhythm, and mostly the same pattern again, turned or mirrored a different
  way. The same sound never gets a pattern one time and a lone note the next. Parts that sound different, like a drop,
  still get completely new patterns
- comes with five example maps (just their notes), which it copies its patterns from:
    jump maps:   Dream Flashback (AshFromKaaba), I will freak you up kid (AshFromKaaba)
    stream maps: Older (WhiteCatte), Prom Night (Gloomywater175)
    quantum:     Ayumi (Teft2oo)
  MORE OPTIONS > Patterns: "Jump map" makes jumpstreams like the jump maps (knight and 2-cell jumps), "Stream map"
  makes lines and circles like the stream maps, and Quantum makes quantum maps like Ayumi: quantum jumps, and slides
  of notes so close together they play as one sweep, out to the edges
- quantum notes can now go out to the edge of the quantum area (-0.85 to 2.85), like the editor allows
- fixed the beat being placed between the drums on songs where the kick doesn't land on every beat (it heard
  "I will freak you up kid" as triplets and put the notes off the beat)

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.7 - 10/16/2026 --

Auto mapper:
- Quantum is real quantum now: quantum jumps and quantum slides (from quantum maps it learned, or made to fit the
  song), never 3x3 grid patterns just moved over a little. Grid patterns are never shifted by part of a cell at all
- the Quantum option really goes for quantum: with it on, almost every note is a quantum jump or slide. Slides go in
  the quick parts, jumps in the slower ones, and they take turns so it doesn't do one thing the whole map
- no more random stops: a bar's notes are spread over the bar instead of bunched at its start, a gap of half a second
  or more is filled in wherever the drums carry on, and the mapping never just ends early
- the options do what they say: Jump Size, Patterns, Variety and Flow change the map far more than before

----------------------------------
   -- Plus 3.0.6 - 10/16/2026 --

- fixed updating: the app closed for the update and never opened again. Updates no longer use a PowerShell script:
  the new version unpacks itself, waits for the old one to close, copies its files over it and opens the app again.
  Every step is written to update-log.txt in the app's data folder (%APPDATA%/Sound Space Quantum Editor Plus)

IF YOU HAVE 3.0.5 OR OLDER: download this version's zip by hand once and unzip it over your old one (or anywhere);
updates install themselves from then on

